=-3B+6 \\\\ 6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3f4ae5f-2a6c-567f-b63c-ad4e4959e404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb8a3d-56a5-5e10-94a4-7af107198885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{7}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278747} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e61a2e-1e40-5e75-b293-68a9203b6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{3}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278748} {:answer/id #uuid "6518ffc0-cff6-5554-9615-49971311022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278749} {:answer/id #uuid "a082b5ef-4432-5a26-94b1-5ee1f5d438d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{1}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6518ffc0-cff6-5554-9615-49971311022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278749} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da4db48-0dde-5e52-941d-16512a829e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae5b3b02-16d6-5d15-8449-764655c69b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11007" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+11$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-22x-59=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+11).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}-22(5)-59 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+11) \\\\ 25-110-59 & =36C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{144}{36} \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-22(0)-59 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+11) \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11C \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11(-4) \\\\ 5B & =-44+59 \\\\ B & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-22(1)-59 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+11) \\\\ 1-22-59 & =-4A-4B+12C \\\\ -80 & =-4A-4(3)+12(-4) \\\\ 4A & =-12-48+80 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=3,$ and $C=-4.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e55f486a-bba8-5b12-8050-b37ad1324a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+9}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{6}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278753} {:answer/id #uuid "16bcf5b2-d66a-54b2-9e2e-c0287929116f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278754} {:answer/id #uuid "3afbe726-981d-5ba5-b433-35e0d24bec16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{7}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278755} {:answer/id #uuid "ed08a7a2-8f22-58ab-9817-a9f9a832e161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{4}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278756} {:answer/id #uuid "8304b930-9e9c-5a18-b212-3b6f09476633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16bcf5b2-d66a-54b2-9e2e-c0287929116f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278754} :answer-field/id #uuid "124787b1-5580-54cb-ac81-a6486c174ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c61f2d91-e7a8-56cf-8315-741d2aa72afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-11013" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-11)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-43x-20=(Ax+B)(x-11)+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=11$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(11)^{2}-43(11)-20 & =(A(11)+B)(11-11)+C((11)^{2}+3) \\\\ 121-473-20 & =124C \\\\ -372 & =124C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{372}{124} \\\\ C & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-43(0)-20 & =(A(0)+B)(0-11)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ -20 & =-11B+3C \\\\ -20 & =-11B+3(-3) \\\\ 11B & =-9+20 \\\\ B & =\\frac{11}{11} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-43(1)-20 & =(A(1)+B)(1-11)+C({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ -62 & =-10A-10B+4C \\\\ -62 & =-10A-10(1)+4(-3) \\\\ 10A & =-10-12+62 \\\\ A & =\\frac{40}{10} \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=4,$ $B=1,$ and $C=-3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)}=\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844192ba-2ead-596b-bbe4-2d01723b0ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) - (3)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093622} {:answer/id #uuid "a0833c98-62bb-5d09-a351-74b96ea5041b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093623} {:answer/id #uuid "ee71b3e3-6b7f-528b-b66f-090604499237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093624} {:answer/id #uuid "30b68480-7100-56df-adce-16e6594ed0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) - (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093625} {:answer/id #uuid "67d9c4d7-b30c-5a90-a731-883e5837afcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (3)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee71b3e3-6b7f-528b-b66f-090604499237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093624} :answer-field/id #uuid "593c74ed-18ae-56d1-b3cd-33c9519975b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ed7374e-1891-5f2a-9591-44dae68b363d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11014" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+2x+5=(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+2(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C((1)^{2}+2(1)+3) \\\\ 5+2+5 & =6C \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({0}^{2})+2(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+2(0)+3) \\\\ 5 & =-B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-B+3(2) \\\\ B & =6-5 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)^{2}+2(2)+5 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+2(2)+3) \\\\ 29 & =2A+B+11C \\\\ 29 & =2A+(1)+11(2) \\\\ 2A & =29-23 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "597bbd6d-c369-53a2-b7c2-caa98f65cfd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278760} {:answer/id #uuid "e27cc934-f5b8-57b0-8a1b-0f041a4e4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278761} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f3fd82-0d58-5910-bd74-06cf4d52f82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278762} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0c5739-0f9e-547a-b9f3-f4fd615b41ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278763} {:answer/id #uuid "7f65bf20-562c-570a-a767-7bb3923b9704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27cc934-f5b8-57b0-8a1b-0f041a4e4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278761} :answer-field/id #uuid "522ba403-f89e-56c6-a15a-20e298d3f6dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d761b54-6320-55f1-b7ed-30330f572d09" :question/math-academy-id "q-49724" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}+3)x}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)x$ and get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+9=(Ax+B)x+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+9 & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 9 & =3C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us an equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+9 & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(3)({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A+B+12 \\\\ A & =-B-1.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us another equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+9 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(3)((-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A-B+12 \\\\ A & =B-1.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the equations $(1)$ and $(2)$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-B-1 & =B-1 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we use our result for $B$ in equation $(2).$ We get\n\n$$\nA=(0)-1=-1.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=0,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-1+0+3=2.\n$$"}]}
